"""Week46 Days 3-4: classification evidence and decision thresholds."""

CONFUSION_EXAMPLE = '''import numpy as np
actual = np.array([0] * 80 + [1] * 20)
predicted = np.array([0] * 76 + [1] * 4 + [0] * 15 + [1] * 5)
'''

L(2, '1. Accuracy needs an error breakdown',
  'Explain why a small accuracy improvement may still miss most positive events.',
  [('Accuracy; error denominator', 'Accuracy is all correct decisions divided by all evaluated cases. Its usefulness depends on class balance and the different consequences of mistakes.'),
   ('False alarm; missed positive', 'A false alarm flags an actual negative. A missed positive fails to flag an actual positive. Overall accuracy combines both with correct decisions.')],
  'Use one hundred invented cases: eighty actual negatives and twenty positives. The proposed classifier catches five positives, misses fifteen and raises four false alarms. It has eighty-one percent accuracy, compared with eighty percent for a never-flag rule. That one-point improvement does not describe the missed-event problem adequately.\n\nAll these counts belong to the same evaluated rows. Do not compare one model’s training accuracy with another model’s validation metric or quietly remove difficult positives. A metric must be tied to its task, denominator and actual label mapping.\n\nAccuracy is not forbidden; it is incomplete on its own. Report it with the confusion counts, event prevalence, precision, recall and a relevant baseline. Different decisions can share the same accuracy and have very different operational consequences. This example uses invented cases for hand calculation, not actual customer evidence.',
  CONFUSION_EXAMPLE + 'print("Model accuracy:", float((actual == predicted).mean()))\nprint("Never-flag accuracy:", float((actual == 0).mean()))\nprint("Positives found:", int(((actual == 1) & (predicted == 1)).sum()))\nprint("Positives missed:", int(((actual == 1) & (predicted == 0)).sum()))\nprint("False alarms:", int(((actual == 0) & (predicted == 1)).sum()))',
  'Compare two decisions with the same correct count but different missed-positive counts.',
  'models = {"A": {"tn": 76, "tp": 5, "fn": 15}, "B": {"tn": 66, "tp": 15, "fn": 5}}\nfor name, c in models.items():\n    print(name, "correct", c["tn"] + c["tp"], "missed positives", c["fn"])',
  'Compute the positive prevalence in the invented80/20evaluation set.',
  'positives, total = 20, 100\nprint("Positive prevalence:", positives / total)',
  [('Does81%accuracy tell you how many cancellations were caught?', 'Not without the error breakdown', 'Always', 'Only with a large training set', 'Overall correctness mixes negative and positive outcomes.'),
   ('Can two models have the same accuracy but different missed-event counts?', 'Yes', 'Never', 'Only if labels are wrong', 'Correct negatives and correct positives can trade off at the same total.')],
  'Treat a one-point accuracy gain as proof that the important event is handled well.', 'State class balance and error counts before interpreting the gain.',
  'The81correct cases are76true negatives plus5true positives. The15missed positives remain visible even though the accuracy is slightly above the never-flag reference.')

L(2, '2. Read every confusion-matrix cell correctly',
  'Construct a labelled matrix and map its cells to TN, FP, FN and TP.',
  [('confusion_matrix; TN/FP/FN/TP', 'With labels=[0,1], rows are actual classes and columns predicted classes: [[TN,FP],[FN,TP]]. Positive means the explicitly declared event.'),
   ('Support; matrix orientation', 'Row totals give actual class counts. Column totals give decision counts. Reading an unlabelled matrix in the opposite orientation reverses the interpretation.')],
  'A confusion matrix connects model decisions to observed outcomes. True negative means actual0and predicted0. False positive means actual0but predicted1. False negative means actual1but predicted0. True positive means actual1and predicted1. Write these meanings before using abbreviations.\n\nSpecify labels=[0,1] even in small subsets so the order remains explicit. The chart labels actual rows and predicted columns. The total of all four cells must equal the number evaluated; the diagonal sum gives correct decisions.\n\nChanging which event is called positive changes which error receives the FP or FN name. Keep the business wording beside the numbers. For this toy example, positive means the event; in the main lab it means cancellation, while the Iris exercise has a different label mapping. Matrix counts are observed sample outcomes, not probabilities or causal explanations.',
  CONFUSION_EXAMPLE + 'from sklearn.metrics import confusion_matrix\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nc = confusion_matrix(actual, predicted, labels=[0, 1])\ntn, fp, fn, tp = c.ravel()\nprint("Matrix:", c.tolist())\nprint("TN FP FN TP:", int(tn), int(fp), int(fn), int(tp))\nprint("Total:", int(c.sum()))\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(5.4, 4.3))\nax.imshow(c, cmap="Blues", vmin=0)\nax.set(xticks=[0, 1], yticks=[0, 1], xticklabels=["Negative", "Positive"], yticklabels=["Negative", "Positive"], xlabel="Predicted decision", ylabel="Actual outcome", title="Read every confusion cell")\nfor i in range(2):\n    for j in range(2):\n        ax.text(j, i, str(c[i, j]), ha="center", va="center", color="white" if c[i, j] > 40 else "black", fontsize=16)\nfig.tight_layout()\nfig.savefig("chart.png", dpi=110)\nplt.close(fig)',
  'Build a matrix for actual[0,0,1,1] and decisions[0,1,0,1].',
  'from sklearn.metrics import confusion_matrix\nc = confusion_matrix([0, 0, 1, 1], [0, 1, 0, 1], labels=[0, 1])\nprint(c.tolist())\nprint("TN FP FN TP:", c.ravel().tolist())',
  'Recover actual-positive support and predicted-positive count from the toy matrix.',
  'tn, fp, fn, tp = 76, 4, 15, 5\nprint("Actual positives:", fn + tp)\nprint("Flagged cases:", fp + tp)\nprint("Correct:", tn + tp)',
  [('Where are false negatives in the declared matrix?', 'Actual-positive row, predicted-negative column', 'Actual-negative row, predicted-positive column', 'Always the diagonal', 'A false negative is a missed actual positive.'),
   ('What do matrix row totals represent here?', 'Actual class support', 'Only the predicted class counts', 'Model confidence', 'Rows correspond to actual labels in the stated orientation.')],
  'Read a matrix without checking label order or which axis is actual.', 'Label both axes and verify the four cells against their definitions.',
  'The matrix is[[76,4],[15,5]]. Its cells sum to100, and76+5gives81correct. The chart preserves the declared actual-row/predicted-column orientation.',
  figure=('An explicitly labelled binary confusion matrix with76TN,4FP,15FNand5TP', 'Rows are actual outcomes; columns are decisions. These100invented cases demonstrate definitions, not measured real customer performance.'))

L(2, '3. Precision and recall answer different questions',
  'Compute the two denominators and explain their practical meaning.',
  [('precision_score; precision', 'Precision=TP/(TP+FP): among flagged cases, how many were actually positive? It describes the reliability of positive decisions in the evaluated sample.'),
   ('recall_score; recall', 'Recall=TP/(TP+FN): among actual positives, how many were found? It is also called true-positive rate or sensitivity.'),
   ('zero_division convention', 'When a denominator is zero, the metric needs an explicit convention. These examples use zero_division=0 and still report the counts; a numerical replacement is not evidence of performance.')],
  'Precision starts from the flagged queue. Recall starts from the actual positive cases. In the toy matrix, five of nine flagged cases are actual positives, while only five of twenty actual positives were found. Those are different denominators and different questions.\n\nHigh precision can coexist with low recall if a model flags only a few easy cases. High recall can coexist with many false alarms if it flags almost everyone. Whether either behaviour is useful depends on the decision context and available capacity, not a universal preference for one metric.\n\nIf no case is flagged, precision has a zero denominator. If a subset has no actual positives, recall does too. Do not hide that context behind a default number. Use an explicit computational convention and report support and decision counts alongside it. Changing prevalence or the evaluated population can also change precision even when class-conditional error rates look similar.',
  CONFUSION_EXAMPLE + 'from sklearn.metrics import precision_score, recall_score\nprint("Manual precision:", round(5 / (5 + 4), 3))\nprint("Manual recall:", round(5 / (5 + 15), 3))\nprint("Library precision:", round(precision_score(actual, predicted, zero_division=0), 3))\nprint("Library recall:", round(recall_score(actual, predicted, zero_division=0), 3))',
  'Compute precision and recall forTP=8,FP=2,FN=12.',
  'tp, fp, fn = 8, 2, 12\nprint("Precision:", tp / (tp + fp))\nprint("Recall:", tp / (tp + fn))',
  'Use an explicit precision convention when no sample is flagged.',
  'from sklearn.metrics import precision_score\ny, p = [0, 0, 1, 1], [0, 0, 0, 0]\nprint("Flagged cases:", sum(p))\nprint("Precision convention:", precision_score(y, p, zero_division=0))\nprint("Report the zero flagged count beside the value")',
  [('What is the denominator of recall?', 'All actual positives', 'All predicted positives', 'All negatives only', 'Recall asks how many true events were found.'),
   ('Does a replacement value for undefined precision create performance evidence?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only if zero_division=0', 'The underlying zero decision count must still be explained.')],
  'Swap precision and recall denominators or silently report undefined cases as success.', 'Name the conditioning group and report the supporting counts.',
  'Precision is5/9and recall is5/20for the same decisions. The explicit zero_division setting avoids unexpected warnings without pretending an empty flagged queue proves high reliability.')

L(2, '4. F1 and balanced accuracy are summaries with limits',
  'Calculate F1 and balanced accuracy and identify what each leaves out.',
  [('f1_score; harmonic mean', 'F1=2*precision*recall/(precision+recall), equivalently2TP/(2TP+FP+FN). It balances those two summaries but does not include true negatives directly.'),
   ('balanced_accuracy_score', 'For this binary task, balanced accuracy is the average of positive recall and negative recall. It gives the two actual classes equal weight regardless of sample counts.')],
  'An arithmetic average of precision and recall can be misleading when one is very small. F1 uses their harmonic mean, so a near-zero component strongly limits the result. Its compact formula is useful, but F1 is not an operational cost model and ignores true negatives directly.\n\nBalanced accuracy averages recall across actual classes. In binary classification it combines TP/(TP+FN) and TN/(TN+FP). A never-positive rule gets positive recall zero and negative recall one, so its binary balanced accuracy is0.5even when ordinary accuracy is very high.\n\nChoose metrics before examining final results. A threshold that maximises F1 need not minimise a declared shortage or false-alarm cost, and an attractive balanced accuracy does not establish calibrated probabilities. Report confusion counts and context rather than treating one combined number as a universal quality certificate.',
  CONFUSION_EXAMPLE + 'from sklearn.metrics import f1_score, balanced_accuracy_score\nmanual_f1 = 2 * 5 / (2 * 5 + 4 + 15)\nmanual_balanced = (5 / 20 + 76 / 80) / 2\nprint("Manual F1:", round(manual_f1, 3))\nprint("Library F1:", round(f1_score(actual, predicted, zero_division=0), 3))\nprint("Manual balanced accuracy:", round(manual_balanced, 3))\nprint("Library balanced accuracy:", round(balanced_accuracy_score(actual, predicted), 3))',
  'Compute balanced accuracy for a never-positive classifier on95negatives and5positives.',
  'from sklearn.metrics import balanced_accuracy_score\ny = [0] * 95 + [1] * 5\np = [0] * 100\nprint("Balanced accuracy:", balanced_accuracy_score(y, p))',
  'Show that adding true negatives does not change F1 when TP/FP/FN are fixed.',
  'tp, fp, fn = 5, 4, 15\nfor tn in [76, 760]:\n    f1 = 2 * tp / (2 * tp + fp + fn)\n    print("TN", tn, "F1", round(f1, 3))',
  [('Does F1 directly include true negatives?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only when labels are0and1', 'Its formula uses TP,FPandFN.'),
   ('Is the F1-optimal threshold always cost-optimal?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only for rare events', 'The two objectives are different and can select different decisions.')],
  'Present one F1 number as the complete business cost and probability assessment.', 'State the summary’s definition and report counts, costs and probability evidence separately.',
  'The formulas and library calculations agree for the same matrix. Balanced accuracy differs from ordinary accuracy because its two class recalls receive equal weight.')

L(2, '5. Report multiclass averages without hiding weak classes',
  'Distinguish macro, weighted and micro summaries and inspect per-class support.',
  [('Macro versus weighted average', 'Macro averages class metrics equally. Weighted averages them by actual class support, so large classes can dominate the result.'),
   ('Micro average; classification_report', 'Micro pools class decisions before calculating a metric. For ordinary single-label multiclass evaluation over all classes, micro precision/recall/F1 equal accuracy. classification_report provides per-class support and summaries.')],
  'Multiclass tasks need a per-class view. A model can handle a large class well while failing on a smaller class. A support-weighted average may still look strong because the large class contributes most of its weight. Macro averaging gives each class equal influence, but also needs context when some classes have very few examples.\n\nUse explicit labels and inspect the individual precision, recall, F1 and support entries. Micro aggregation is another definition, not a universally better metric. Its equality with accuracy here depends on one label per sample and evaluating all classes; it does not transfer unchanged to arbitrary multilabel or partial-class evaluations.\n\nThe invented three-class example makes the small-class problem visible. The main churn project remains binary with one declared positive class, so its default precision/recall refer to cancellation. Do not mix a binary positive-class metric with a multiclass macro metric without naming the different definitions.',
  'from sklearn.metrics import f1_score, classification_report, accuracy_score\ny = [0] * 8 + [1] * 2 + [2] * 2\np = [0] * 8 + [0] * 2 + [1] * 2\nfor average in ["macro", "weighted", "micro"]:\n    print(average, "F1", round(f1_score(y, p, average=average, labels=[0, 1, 2], zero_division=0), 3))\nr = classification_report(y, p, labels=[0, 1, 2], output_dict=True, zero_division=0)\nfor label in ["0", "1", "2"]:\n    print("Class", label, "support", int(r[label]["support"]), "recall", round(r[label]["recall"], 3))\nprint("Accuracy:", round(accuracy_score(y, p), 3))',
  'Compute an equal-class and support-weighted mean of class recalls[1,0] with supports[90,10].',
  'import numpy as np\nrecalls, support = np.array([1.0, 0.0]), np.array([90, 10])\nprint("Macro recall:", float(recalls.mean()))\nprint("Support-weighted recall:", float(np.average(recalls, weights=support)))',
  'Identify the class with the smallest actual support in a report table.',
  'support = {"A": 100, "B": 20, "C": 3}\nprint("Smallest class:", min(support, key=support.get))\nprint("Inspect its counts before trusting a summary")',
  [('Which average gives each class equal weight?', 'Macro', 'Support-weighted', 'Always ordinary accuracy', 'Macro ignores class-size weights when averaging class metrics.'),
   ('Can a weighted average hide a poorly handled small class?', 'Yes', 'Never', 'Only when probabilities are missing', 'Large support can dominate the aggregate.')],
  'Publish a weighted summary without the per-class support or failure pattern.', 'State the averaging convention and include class-level evidence.',
  'The majority class is handled well while both smaller classes have zero recall. The different aggregate F1 values expose how weighting changes the reported story.')

L(2, '6. Write a metric report tied to actual decisions',
  'Connect scores, a threshold, confusion counts and declared evidence limits.',
  [('binary_metrics', 'The supplied helper computes binary counts, threshold metrics, ranking metrics, probability error and illustrative cost. It requires paired scores and both actual classes0/1; its positive decision is score>=threshold.'),
   ('Metric provenance', 'A score needs the evaluated sample, label mapping, threshold and denominator to be interpretable. A final report must distinguish development evidence from reserved-test evidence.')],
  'A useful report starts with the question and actual label meanings. State which rows were evaluated, how the model was fitted, the decision threshold and the confusion counts. Then precision, recall, F1 and accuracy can be checked against the underlying evidence.\n\nThe helper also returns ranking and probability summaries, developed in Day4 and the calibration discussion. These are different views of the same score vector; they do not excuse omitting the actual decision counts. The lab’s computational zero-denominator convention is documented and paired with support.\n\nThe example evaluates a training-fitted model on validation only. This is development evidence, not the final project report. All source rows remain in the evaluation; difficult cases are not removed to improve the number. A good report distinguishes observed counts, methodological assumptions and external claims that remain unsupported by a simulator.',
  TRAIN_LOGISTIC + 'from lab import binary_metrics\nr = binary_metrics(d["y_valid"], p, threshold=0.5)\nfor key in ["rows", "threshold", "tn", "fp", "fn", "tp", "accuracy", "precision", "recall", "f1"]:\n    print(key, round(r[key], 3) if isinstance(r[key], float) else r[key])\nprint("Evidence: validation on simulated subscriptions")',
  'Recompute accuracy and recall from a returned metric dictionary.',
  TRAIN_LOGISTIC + 'from lab import binary_metrics\nr = binary_metrics(d["y_valid"], p)\nprint("Count accuracy:", round((r["tn"] + r["tp"]) / r["rows"], 6))\nprint("Count recall:", round(r["tp"] / (r["tp"] + r["fn"]), 6))',
  'List four facts that should accompany an accuracy number.',
  'for field in ["label meaning", "evaluated support", "decision threshold", "false positives and false negatives"]:\n    print(field)',
  [('Is a validation report automatically the final untouched test?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only if accuracy is high', 'Its role depends on whether it guides development.'),
   ('What makes a reported precision checkable?', 'TPandFPcounts plus the declared labels and decisions', 'Only the model brand', 'Only the training seed', 'The numerator and denominator must connect to evaluated outcomes.')],
  'Report a floating-point metric with no label mapping, threshold or sample definition.', 'Attach the metric to paired outcomes, decision rules and an explicit evaluation role.',
  'Every displayed metric comes from the same validation score vector and0.5decision rule. The helper’s counts allow independent reconstruction of the headline ratios.')

L(3, '1. Evaluate ranking with continuous scores',
  'Interpret ROC AUC as a pairwise ranking summary and handle ties explicitly.',
  [('ROC AUC; roc_auc_score', 'For binary labels, ROC AUC measures how often a randomly selected positive scores above a negative, with half credit for a tied pair. Use continuous scores, not only hard decisions.'),
   ('Ranking versus operating point', 'A ranking summary spans score thresholds. It does not specify which threshold to use, how many cases will be flagged or whether probabilities are calibrated.')],
  'Imagine comparing every positive case with every negative case. A model gets a win when the positive has the larger score and half a win for a tie. ROC AUC summarises this ranking relationship. A value near0.5indicates chance-like ordering in expectation, not a guaranteed result for every finite random sample.\n\nPassing only hard labels collapses a rich ordering into two levels. You can calculate an AUC for that collapsed vector, but it no longer answers the full ranking question. Supply predict_proba’s positive column or an appropriate continuous decision score and document the positive class.\n\nROC AUC does not tell you decision cost, positive precision at a chosen threshold or calibration. It also needs both actual classes for this binary interpretation; a subset containing only one class cannot estimate positive-versus-negative ranking. The small example calculates pairwise credit directly so the library result is understandable.',
  'import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.metrics import roc_auc_score\ny = np.array([0, 0, 1, 1])\np = np.array([0.1, 0.7, 0.8, 0.4])\npositive, negative = p[y == 1], p[y == 0]\ncredit = [float(a > b) + 0.5 * float(a == b) for a in positive for b in negative]\nprint("Pairwise credit:", credit)\nprint("Manual AUC:", float(np.mean(credit)))\nprint("Library AUC:", roc_auc_score(y, p))\nprint("AUC after hard decisions:", roc_auc_score(y, p >= 0.5))',
  'Compute half credit for a tied positive/negative pair.',
  'from sklearn.metrics import roc_auc_score\nprint("One tied pair AUC:", roc_auc_score([0, 1], [0.4, 0.4]))',
  'Detect that a one-class subset cannot support this binary ranking comparison.',
  'labels = [0, 0, 0]\nprint("Both classes present:", set(labels) == {0, 1})\nprint("Do not fabricate an AUC when one comparison class is absent")',
  [('What information should usually be passed for ROC AUC?', 'Continuous positive scores or appropriate decision scores', 'Only the selected class labels', 'Feature row ids', 'Ranking needs the score ordering rather than one threshold’s decisions.'),
   ('Does ROC AUC choose an operational threshold?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only for logistic regression', 'Threshold choice requires a separate decision criterion.')],
  'Treat a high AUC as a calibrated probability or a ready-made action rule.', 'Use it as ranking evidence and evaluate the chosen decision separately.',
  'Three of the four positive-negative pairs are correctly ordered, giving0.75. The hard-label vector discards within-group score order, so its AUC can differ.')

L(3, '2. Read ROC and precision-recall curves together',
  'Plot validation curves and explain their axes, prevalence and summary definitions.',
  [('roc_curve; false-positive rate', 'ROC plots true-positive rate against FP/(FP+TN) across score thresholds. Its initial threshold can be infinity for an always-negative starting point.'),
   ('precision_recall_curve; average_precision_score', 'A PR curve plots precision against recall. Average precision summarises precision weighted by recall increments; it is not simply trapezoidal area under a linearly interpolated PR plot.'),
   ('PR endpoint convention', 'precision_recall_curve returns one more precision/recall point than thresholds. Its final point has no corresponding threshold; do not blindly zip all arrays as equal-length records.')],
  'ROC shows the tradeoff between finding positives and flagging actual negatives. On a very imbalanced population, a small false-positive rate can still correspond to many false alarms because the negative denominator is large. Read counts and the operating threshold alongside the curve.\n\nPrecision-recall focuses on the positive decisions and found events. Precision depends on prevalence; the dashed prevalence line is a useful no-skill reference in expectation, not proof that every random sample must follow it. Average precision is a declared summary distinct from a trapezoidal interpolation calculation.\n\nThe two plots use the same training-fitted model and untouched-by-fit validation score vector. They are development evidence. The final subscription test remains aside. Neither curve certifies calibration, actual business value or performance on another population. The code checks array lengths so the special PR endpoint cannot be mistaken for a thresholded decision.',
  TRAIN_LOGISTIC + 'from sklearn.metrics import roc_curve, precision_recall_curve, roc_auc_score, average_precision_score\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\ny = d["y_valid"]\nfpr, tpr, rt = roc_curve(y, p)\nprecision, recall, pt = precision_recall_curve(y, p)\nprint("Validation ROC AUC:", round(roc_auc_score(y, p), 3))\nprint("Validation average precision:", round(average_precision_score(y, p), 3))\nprint("PR points / thresholds:", len(precision), len(pt))\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(2, 1, figsize=(5.6, 7))\naxes[0].plot(fpr, tpr, color="#376ea6", label="Logistic validation ranking")\naxes[0].plot([0, 1], [0, 1], linestyle="--", color="#888888")\naxes[0].set(xlabel="False-positive rate", ylabel="Recall / true-positive rate", title="ROC: validate the ranking")\naxes[1].step(recall, precision, where="post", color="#3d927a")\naxes[1].axhline(float(y.mean()), linestyle="--", color="#888888", label="Validation prevalence")\naxes[1].set(xlabel="Recall", ylabel="Precision", title="PR: inspect the positive-event tradeoff", ylim=(0, 1.05))\naxes[1].legend(fontsize=9)\nfig.tight_layout()\nfig.savefig("chart.png", dpi=110)\nplt.close(fig)',
  'Verify the PR endpoint length rule on a small paired score vector.',
  'from sklearn.metrics import precision_recall_curve\nprecision, recall, thresholds = precision_recall_curve([0, 0, 1, 1], [0.1, 0.7, 0.8, 0.4])\nprint("Points:", len(precision), len(recall))\nprint("Thresholds:", len(thresholds))\nprint("Thresholded points:", len(precision[:-1]) == len(thresholds))',
  'Convert a one-percent false-positive rate into a count for ten thousand negatives.',
  'negatives, fpr = 10000, 0.01\nprint("False alarms:", int(negatives * fpr))\nprint("A small rate can still create a substantial queue")',
  [('Does a small false-positive rate always mean few false alarms?', 'No; the negative count matters', 'Always', 'Only when AUC is high', 'Rate multiplied by its denominator gives the count.'),
   ('Does every precision-recall point have its own threshold value?', 'No; the final endpoint has none', 'Always', 'Only when scores are tied', 'The API returns one extra PR endpoint.')],
  'Use ROC alone on a rare event and treat PR arrays as identical-length threshold records.', 'Read both curves, prevalence, counts and the documented endpoint conventions.',
  'Both panels come from the same validation outcomes and continuous scores. The printed summaries name ROC AUC and average precision separately, and the length check exposes the extra PR endpoint.',
  figure=('Validation ROC and precision-recall curves for the same fitted logistic model', 'Top: recall versus false-positive rate. Bottom: precision versus recall with validation prevalence shown. Curves are development evidence, not calibrated probabilities or final test results.'))

L(3, '3. Move a threshold and count what changes',
  'Track false alarms and missed events while keeping the score vector fixed.',
  [('Threshold operating point', 'A chosen threshold selects one confusion matrix from a fixed score vector. Lowering the lab threshold cannot reduce flagged count, but precision need not improve monotonically.'),
   ('Threshold-invariant ranking', 'For the same continuous score vector, changing only the decision threshold does not change ROC AUC or average precision.')],
  'Lowering a threshold makes it easier to flag a case. On fixed rows this cannot reduce true positives or false positives, though precision can move irregularly as different cases enter the queue. Raising the threshold reduces the queue and can miss more events.\n\nDo not describe a threshold change as learning a new ranking. The scores remain the same; only the action rule changes. ROC AUC and average precision computed from those unchanged scores remain unchanged, while accuracy, precision, recall, F1 and costs can differ.\n\nThe example reads several thresholds on validation for development. It does not select one by final-test performance. A threshold has no universal meaning independent of score calibration, prevalence and the task’s consequences. The next parts declare a simple illustrative cost and use it to make the selection rule explicit.',
  TRAIN_LOGISTIC + 'from lab import binary_metrics\nfor threshold in [0.1, 0.3, 0.5]:\n    r = binary_metrics(d["y_valid"], p, threshold)\n    print("Threshold", threshold, "FP", r["fp"], "FN", r["fn"], "precision", round(r["precision"], 3), "recall", round(r["recall"], 3), "AUC", round(r["roc_auc"], 3))',
  'Count flags for the same scores at thresholds0.2and0.6.',
  'import numpy as np\np = np.array([0.1, 0.3, 0.5, 0.8])\nfor t in [0.2, 0.6]:\n    print(t, "flagged:", int((p >= t).sum()))',
  'Verify that a lower threshold’s flagged set contains a higher threshold’s set.',
  'import numpy as np\np = np.array([0.1, 0.3, 0.5, 0.8])\nlow = set(np.flatnonzero(p >= 0.2))\nhigh = set(np.flatnonzero(p >= 0.6))\nprint("Higher-threshold flags included:", high <= low)',
  [('Can precision fluctuate as thresholds change?', 'Yes', 'Never', 'Only when fitting fails', 'The composition of newly included cases can change precision irregularly.'),
   ('Does changing only a threshold alter ROC AUC of the original score vector?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only at0.5', 'AUC is computed from score ordering, which was not changed.')],
  'Say lowering the threshold improved the model’s AUC while using exactly the same scores.', 'Separate ranking evidence from the consequences of the chosen action rule.',
  'The same p and actual validation labels are reused. Confusion counts change with threshold, while the printed AUC repeats because the score ordering did not change.')

L(3, '4. Declare a cost objective before selecting actions',
  'Compute an explicit false-positive/false-negative tradeoff without inventing real losses.',
  [('Illustrative decision cost', 'This lab assigns1unit to a false positive and8units to a false negative, with zero cost for correct decisions. These are teaching weights, not observed customer or financial losses.'),
   ('Cost versus F1 objective', 'A threshold minimising weighted mistake cost need not maximise F1, accuracy or recall. The selection criterion must be stated before final results are inspected.')],
  'A threshold decision needs a purpose. For this exercise, a false alarm costs one illustrative unit and a missed cancellation costs eight. Total cost is FP+8×FN, and dividing by the evaluated count gives cost per row. Those weights are authored assumptions, not measurements of a real service.\n\nA real decision could include capacity limits, intervention effects, false-alarm burden and costs even for correctly flagged cases. This deliberately simple matrix does not model those details. A cancellation predictor alone also cannot prove that contacting a flagged account prevents cancellation.\n\nThe example compares two invented confusion profiles. A profile can have more false alarms and still lower declared cost if it prevents enough costly misses. That is why selecting by accuracy after declaring a different cost would be inconsistent. Use validation to choose the action rule and reserve final cases to report the frozen procedure.',
  'from lab import FP_COST, FN_COST\nprofiles = {"cautious flags": {"fp": 4, "fn": 15}, "more flags": {"fp": 20, "fn": 5}}\nfor name, r in profiles.items():\n    cost = FP_COST * r["fp"] + FN_COST * r["fn"]\n    print(name, "teaching cost", cost, "units")\nprint("Assumed FP/FN weights:", FP_COST, FN_COST)',
  'Compute cost per row forFP=10,FN=3and100evaluated cases under the declared weights.',
  'fp, fn, rows = 10, 3, 100\ncost = fp + 8 * fn\nprint("Total:", cost)\nprint("Per row:", cost / rows)',
  'State why finding likely cancellations does not prove an intervention works.',
  'print("Prediction: who may cancel under the observed setting")\nprint("Causal question: what changes if an intervention is applied")\nprint("The first does not establish the second")',
  [('Must the cost-optimal threshold also maximise F1?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only for binary labels', 'The objectives weight outcomes differently.'),
   ('Are the1and8weights measured real business losses?', 'No; they are explicit teaching assumptions', 'Yes', 'Only after a model fits', 'The report distinguishes an assumed objective from observed evidence.')],
  'Present arbitrary cost weights as measured business value or a proven retention intervention.', 'Name the assumed costs and the causal questions the model does not answer.',
  'The first profile costs124units and the second60under the declared rule. Extra false alarms can be worthwhile within that illustrative objective, not automatically in a real system.')

L(3, '5. Select a threshold using validation only',
  'Apply the declared grid and deterministic cost tie-break while keeping final labels aside.',
  [('THRESHOLDS; choose_threshold', 'The lab declares a grid from0to1plus1.01for never flagging. choose_threshold minimises validation cost and prefers the larger threshold for an exact cost tie.'),
   ('Selection bias', 'Trying many thresholds on one validation sample can exploit its noise. The selected validation score is development evidence; a reserved final evaluation is still needed.')],
  'Score validation rows with the training-fitted model, then evaluate the declared threshold grid on those same validation outcomes. Select the minimum illustrative cost, with the documented tie-break. Do not inspect final-test labels to choose either the grid or the winning point.\n\nThe1.01sentinel is an explicit never-flag candidate because valid probability scores cannot exceed1. Threshold0flags every case under the lab’s>=rule. Including these endpoints makes the comparison transparent rather than silently assuming an intervention always helps.\n\nValidation selection does not guarantee future improvement. A finer grid or more candidate models can overfit development feedback. The final project therefore freezes both the fitted candidate and selected threshold before reporting its reserved test. More extensive nested evaluation belongs to Week47; this week makes the basic boundary visible.',
  TRAIN_LOGISTIC + 'from lab import choose_threshold, binary_metrics\nselected = choose_threshold(d["y_valid"], p)\ndefault = binary_metrics(d["y_valid"], p, 0.5)\nprint("Selected validation threshold:", round(selected["threshold"], 3))\nprint("Selected validation cost:", selected["total_cost"])\nprint("At0.5cost:", default["total_cost"])\nprint("Final test consulted: False")',
  'Apply the cost/larger-threshold tie-break to an invented development table.',
  'rows = [{"threshold": 0.1, "cost": 40}, {"threshold": 0.2, "cost": 40}, {"threshold": 0.5, "cost": 60}]\nchoice = min(rows, key=lambda r: (r["cost"], -r["threshold"]))\nprint(choice)',
  'Verify the never-flag sentinel on probability endpoints0and1.',
  'import numpy as np\np = np.array([0.0, 1.0])\nprint("Flags at1.01:", (p >= 1.01).astype(int).tolist())\nprint("Flags at0:", (p >= 0).astype(int).tolist())',
  [('Where must the threshold be selected in this project?', 'Validation', 'Final test labels', 'After choosing the nicest final score', 'The final test must not guide the action rule.'),
   ('Why is1.01included?', 'It explicitly represents never flagging', 'It is a valid probability estimate', 'It calibrates the model', 'Valid probabilities are at most1, so the declared comparison is always false.')],
  'Tune the threshold on final labels and report its same-sample cost as untouched evidence.', 'Declare the objective and grid, select with validation and evaluate the frozen result later.',
  'The selected cost is compared with the default on validation only. The function chooses a decision threshold; it never refits or accesses the model’s final-test outcomes.')

L(3, '6. Freeze the fit and threshold together',
  'Explain why a silent refit after threshold selection can change the decision procedure.',
  [('Frozen model-threshold pair', 'The threshold was chosen for the scores of a particular training fit. The lab retains that fit rather than silently refitting on training plus validation before final scoring.'),
   ('Development reuse versus final evidence', 'Using one validation set for multiple choices is allowed as development but can overfit. Final reporting must acknowledge that reuse and remain independent of final-label choices.')],
  'A threshold belongs to a score-producing procedure. Refitting a model on a larger dataset can change its coefficients and probability distribution; keeping the old threshold after that change is a different procedure. It may work, but it needs an appropriate declared evaluation rather than an invisible shortcut.\n\nThis lab chooses a candidate and threshold on validation while keeping each candidate fitted on training only. It then reports the selected pair on the final test. No extra fit occurs on final or validation labels. This simple design makes the boundary easy to audit, though one validation set still supports several choices and can be overused.\n\nThe example checks that threshold selection leaves the scaler state unchanged. That is one implementation check, not proof that every possible source of leakage is absent. Record the full workflow and what was inspected. Once final errors guide a revision, obtain fresh evaluation evidence for later claims.',
  TRAIN_LOGISTIC + 'import numpy as np\nfrom lab import choose_threshold\nbefore = m.named_steps["scale"].mean_.copy()\nselected = choose_threshold(d["y_valid"], p)\nprint("Threshold:", round(selected["threshold"], 3))\nprint("Scaler state unchanged:", bool(np.array_equal(before, m.named_steps["scale"].mean_)))\nprint("Training fit rows:", int(m.named_steps["scale"].n_samples_seen_))',
  'Write the ordered final-evaluation protocol without a silent post-selection fit.',
  'steps = ["fit candidates on train", "select model and threshold on validation", "freeze selected pair", "report final test", "document limitations"]\nfor number, step in enumerate(steps, 1):\n    print(number, step)',
  'Reject an experiment log that tunes again after reading final errors.',
  'actions = ["select validation", "freeze", "read final test", "change threshold"]\nprint("Final feedback used for revision:", actions.index("change threshold") > actions.index("read final test"))',
  [('Can a new fit change the score distribution associated with a selected threshold?', 'Yes', 'Never', 'Only for random forests', 'Fitted parameters and therefore probabilities can change.'),
   ('Does a repeatable final test become fresh after tuning from its errors?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only if the output folder changes', 'Repetition is not independent evaluation.')],
  'Refit invisibly after threshold selection or keep retuning from final errors.', 'Declare the full fitted procedure and preserve the final reporting boundary.',
  'The selected threshold is a scalar decision setting. The scaler count and unchanged mean verify that this selection did not refit the720-row training model.')
